        body: p(`
A meeting may end at ten in the morning, yet its material may not reach the editor until the afternoon. During those hours the journalist was not writing slowly, the newsroom was not reviewing the copy, and it may not even have known that the story existed. The details were still scattered across the institution, waiting to become complete enough to edit. When that finally happens, writing the story may take no more than ten minutes. In the production log, those minutes look like the entire life of the story, while the hours that came before them disappear from the calculation.

The life of a story does not begin when the editor opens the file. Before that, a meeting summary may have arrived in a WhatsApp group, photographs by email, a correction to a name in a private message, and the final wording of a statement in a new document, while an important number remains inside a voice note that not everyone has heard. Each channel may shorten transmission time on its own, yet the multiplication of channels can make access itself more complicated. The task is no longer simply to move details, but to know where they are, what has been completed, and who is waiting for the next step. A study published in Digital Journalism, a peer-reviewed academic journal specializing in digital journalism, drew on seven months of ethnographic work in two Chilean newsrooms and found that WhatsApp affected journalists’ relationships with sources, how they obtained information, and the rhythm of daily work. Technology brought people closer together without necessarily creating a single path for what they exchanged.

The effect of that fragmentation becomes visible when versions begin to multiply. One file sits with the editor, another with the manager who reviewed it, and a third has already reached the designer before the latest amendment. The question is no longer “Where is the story?” but “Which story?” Every version may have been correct at the moment it was sent, until a changed number, name or quote makes only one of them fit for publication. If identifying the approved version depends on someone saying “send me the latest copy,” part of the safety of the workflow has been stored in people’s memories and inboxes instead of being visible to everyone.

More deeply, the order in which information arrives can enter the text itself. A statement that arrives early may give the editor an initial structure, then a more important fact appears after a preliminary angle has already formed and is treated as an addition, even though it might have led the story had it been present from the start. This approaches what psychology calls the “primacy effect”: the tendency for information presented first to have a stronger presence in memory and potentially influence later judgments. Primacy does not determine an editor’s decision, but it helps explain why the sequence of inputs is not always neutral and why information flow itself becomes part of the editorial process.

From this perspective, some measures of speed are incomplete. An organization may know when editing began, how long review took, and the hour at which the text was published, while remaining unable to see how much time passed between the event and the point at which its material became complete inside the organization. If measurement begins when the first piece of information enters the system rather than when the file reaches the journalist, an entire stage that had been outside the metric becomes visible. The time spent moving between stages then becomes a performance indicator in its own right, not merely the time a journalist spends writing.

Fixing this does not require adding another channel to the old ones. It requires a single reference that accompanies the story from the beginning. That principle already has a technical foundation in news production. NewsML-G2 is an open standard for exchanging news content and metadata issued by the International Press Telecommunications Council (IPTC), which develops technical specifications for the news industry. It requires each news item to have an identifier that remains globally unique and independent of where the content is stored, supports version numbering when content is updated, and includes planning elements that can track what was intended for coverage and what has been completed. The idea is to preserve the identity of a story as it moves across systems and stages instead of allowing every new file to become a separate object from the one before it.

Internally, that logic can become a central record opened as soon as the first information about an event arrives: a fixed reference number, source, arrival time, available and missing elements, current owner, and status. Text, images and quotes do not travel as competing copies; they are attached to the same item, with a history of changes and who made them. The open-source newsroom management platform Superdesk offers a practical example of this model by allowing content from different sources to move through configurable stages such as ingest, work, approval and publishing while keeping the production flow visible inside one system.

Centralization does not mean creating another gate at which everyone must wait. One branch can add a photograph, another department can complete a number, and a spokesperson can approve a quote while the journalist begins with what has already been verified. Even breaking material can move forward while clearly marked as incomplete, with a visible account of what is missing and who is responsible for supplying it. The difference is that the gap becomes known and ownership of it becomes explicit instead of being discovered by chance when the editor reaches an empty place in the story.

The workflow data can then move from internal documentation to diagnosis. If dozens of stories repeatedly stop at the same point, the problem is no longer an isolated incident. Consistent delays in photographs point to a weakness in how they are collected. A pile-up of stories at a single approval level may require a review of authority distribution. A routine story taking the same path as a highly sensitive one means the organization is applying one procedure to very different levels of risk. The workflow creating the bottleneck can then be repaired instead of asking everyone at the end of it to work faster. Tracking adds another benefit: the origin of a number, the source of an image, the date of an amendment and the person who approved it stop being fragments scattered across old conversations and become part of institutional memory.

By the time a story reaches the editor, the visible part of its production is only beginning, while earlier stages may already have consumed part of its useful life without appearing in the published version. Between the event and the first line lies a journey that can determine when the story is released, which version is used and which fact leads. Sometimes a story does not die on the journalist’s desk; it arrives there after part of its life has already been lost.
        `)
