        body: p(`
An organization may publish the same story in Arabic, English and French at the same moment. The event, image and names have not changed, and even the number of paragraphs may be similar. Yet an Arabic reader may see that a party “called for” an action, a reader in another language may understand that it “urged” it, and a third may encounter wording that makes the position sound more decisive than it was in the original. A difference does not need to be a glaring error to have an effect. Sometimes a dictionary-correct word is enough when it does not carry the same force in context.

That is why journalistic translation does not work like a machine replacing one word with another. When a news story is translated, it enters a new linguistic system with its own ways of expressing certainty and possibility, responsibility and commitment, and the distance between a speaker and what they say. “Commitment” and “pledge” are not identical in every context, just as “urge” and “request” do not necessarily carry the same degree of insistence. The difference may look small when searching for an Arabic equivalent, but it becomes important when it changes the level of certainty, the strength of a request or the extent to which a speaker owns a position. The question is no longer simply whether the translation is understandable. It is whether the reader in the second language received the meaning with the same semantic force it had in the first.

A study published in Journalism, a peer-reviewed academic journal specializing in journalism studies, drew on in-depth interviews conducted by researcher Alicia Wright with Indian journalists in New Delhi in 2018 and 2019 and examined how they handled the move from a spoken Hindi interview to a written English story. It showed that the journalist does more than transfer words. They negotiate what has a clear equivalent, what does not move easily into the other language, and how the material is presented to a new linguistic audience. In that journey from source to reader, meaning can acquire implications that were not present to the same degree in the first language. Another study published in Journalism Studies in 2024 reinforces this editorial nature of translation. Examining the Urdu service of United News of India, it concluded that “news translation” is an important, if contested, component of media work in Indian-language journalism.

These differences become more sensitive in diplomatic and legal coverage. A declared position is not a pledge, a pledge is not a binding decision, and the official name of a body or a legal status is not merely a label that can be replaced by something that sounds linguistically close. If those levels are flattened while moving between languages, the basic facts may remain correct while what they mean changes. The organization is not publishing three lies; it is publishing three unequal versions of a position that was supposed to be one.

Quotation is even more sensitive because readers assume it is the part of the story closest to the speaker’s own voice. Reuters journalistic standards state that translated quotes should read naturally in the new language without becoming stiffly literal while preserving the tone of the original. The agency specifically warns against translating a quotation back into its first language after it has already passed through another outlet in a different language, because the result can move far from what was originally said. Literalness is not a guarantee of accuracy, and fluency is not one either. The wording should change as much as the new language requires without changing the speaker’s tone or the meaning of the statement.

The distance grows when the editor is not translating from the original source. A statement delivered in Arabic may be carried by one organization in English, then a newsroom may use that English version to produce a story in French—or even back in Arabic. Each stage may look reasonable on its own, but the chain adds another intermediary between the reader and the original words. If the source disappears from the editor’s view, it becomes difficult to know whether the phrase in front of them was the speaker’s choice or the previous translator’s. Translation then moves more than language; it carries editorial decisions whose starting point later editors may no longer be able to see.

Artificial intelligence did not create this problem. A skilled human translator can choose an equivalent unsuited to an institutional context, just as an automated tool can produce a grammatically correct sentence while dropping a fine distinction between two concepts. Organizations that deal with sensitive language every day do not rely on individual linguistic knowledge alone. The United Nations uses UNTERM for official terminology alongside computer-assisted translation tools, text alignment systems and document repositories. Its translators and editors research new terminology, verify it against reliable sources and consult specialists before approved terms are stored. The tools support consistency and accuracy, but they do not eliminate the need for human judgment capable of understanding meaning, tone and even deliberate ambiguity in some diplomatic texts.

A newsroom can borrow that logic without becoming an international translation department. Instead of each editor beginning from their own dictionary and memory, the organization can build a living editorial glossary for recurring terms in its coverage: the original expression, the approved equivalent in each language, the context in which it applies, the official source for legal and institutional names, and warnings about alternatives that look similar but change the meaning. When a new usage appears or an official name changes, the same record is updated rather than every translation starting from zero.

Terminology alone, however, cannot protect the story because a sentence is also shaped by what surrounds it. Sensitive parts of a story therefore need an anchor that always points back to the source. A quotation is linked to the recording or first text, an official name to its authoritative document, and an important diplomatic phrase should not travel from one translation to another when the original is available. If two editors disagree over an equivalent, the question is not “Which wording sounds nicer?” but “Which wording preserves the function the word had in the original?”

Technology can accelerate that review without carrying it out alone. Before publication, the most sensitive elements across versions can be compared and differences in negation, degree of certainty, institutional names, approved terminology or the meaning of translated quotations can be flagged. Not every difference is an error; languages are not written from identical templates. The purpose of comparison is to make the difference visible before publication. If the editor knows the source language, they can inspect it directly. If not, the disputed point can be sent to someone who can return to the original and determine whether the variation is a linguistic necessity or a change in meaning.

The deeper problem appears when differences become patterns. If the same institution is consistently described by its official name in one language and an unapproved name in another, or if cautious formulations repeatedly become more decisive when moved into a particular language, these are no longer scattered translator slips. Each linguistic track has developed its own way of interpreting the source, and over time the organization begins producing different versions of a single reality it is supposed to describe.

A reader who chooses Arabic, English or French does not expect to choose a different degree of truth with the language. It is natural for sentences to change so they read properly in each language. What should not change is the level of certainty, the force of a position, the identity of who did what, and the boundaries of what was actually said. It is not enough for all three versions to look as though each was originally written in its own language. Successful journalistic translation means readers in all three languages arrive at the same truth, even if they reach it through different words.
        `)
